This is actually **more informative than the “both embeddings give the same thing” result I was half-expecting.** SIM14.2 found a very precise distinction.

The headline is:

$$
\boxed{
G_1\cong G_2\cong(\mathbb Z_2)^3
\quad\text{as abstract groups,}
}
$$

but

$$
\boxed{
G_1\curvearrowright C_8
\quad\text{and}\quad
G_2\curvearrowright C_8
\text{ are fundamentally different actions.}
}
$$

There is a small terminology correction I'd make to our generated truth packet because the output makes this clearer than our preregistered label does. **M1 does generate \((\mathbb Z_2)^3\).** Both branches commute, both have group order 8, and both have seven nonidentity elements of order 2. 

What M1 fails is **regularity of the action on the eight states**.

That's the important result.

### M1 fractures the eight-state carrier

For M1, every vertex has

$$
|\operatorname{Orb}(v)|=4,\qquad
|\operatorname{Stab}(v)|=2,
$$

and the carrier separates into two invariant four-state orbits:

$$
\boxed{
\{0,1,6,7\}
\sqcup
\{2,3,4,5\}.
}
$$

M2 instead has

$$
|\operatorname{Orb}(v)|=8,\qquad
|\operatorname{Stab}(v)|=1,
$$

for every vertex. It acts transitively and freely—hence regularly—over the complete eight-state carrier. 

That is a beautiful distinction because the abstract group didn't change:

$$
(\mathbb Z_2)^3
$$

in both cases.

**Its realization did.**

---

### And we can see exactly why

Look at M1's binary element `101`:

$$
ps_{\Omega,1}=(25)(34).
$$

And `111`:

$$
phs_{\Omega,1}=(07)(16).
$$

Those are nonidentity group elements with fixed points. 

Therefore the action cannot be free.

For M2, by contrast, every nonidentity direction is a fixed-point-free four-pair involution:

$$
(02)(13)(46)(57),\quad
(07)(16)(25)(34),\quad\dots
$$

including

$$
(04)(15)(26)(37).
$$



So we've uncovered a very simple structural discriminator:

$$
\boxed{
\text{M2: every nonzero binary direction moves every microscopic state.}
}
$$

whereas

$$
\boxed{
\text{M1: some nonzero binary directions act trivially on part of the carrier.}
}
$$

That's much more concrete than saying one embedding merely “looks nicer.”

### There's another subtle result I really like

M1 **still contains the abstract Fano incidence geometry**.

It has seven \(V_4\) subgroups, and its abstract subgroup-incidence test still passes \(PG(2,2)\).  

But its realization on the eight microscopic states breaks.

Instead of seven clean \(4+4\) sector decompositions, M1 produces only six distinct partitions and not all are \(4+4\). M2 gives all seven distinct \(4+4\) decompositions. 

So SIM14.2 has separated three mathematical layers that we'd previously been discussing almost as one thing:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ccc}
\text{abstract group}
&
\longrightarrow
&
\text{abstract incidence}
\\[2mm]
(\mathbb Z_2)^3
&&
PG(2,2)
\\[3mm]
&&\downarrow\\
&&
\text{action on the 8-state carrier}.
\end{array}}
$$

Both M1 and M2 survive the first two.

**Only M2 gives the regular eight-state realization.**

That is a much better understanding of what SIM14.1 actually discovered.

### And the frozen projection \(p\) matters

There's another result worth preserving.

Under bare

$$
\operatorname{Aut}(C_8),
$$

M1 and M2 themselves are equivalent: eight graph automorphisms exchange them.

But once we demand preservation of the already-frozen projection pairing \(p\),

$$
\boxed{M_1\not\sim M_2.}
$$



That's almost exactly the distinction we've been circling since SIM13:

$$
\boxed{
\text{bare carrier equivalence}
\neq
\text{relational equivalence after additional structure is fixed}.
}
$$

And we didn't need ISP/LCO to manufacture that statement. It already exists statically.

---

I would therefore slightly rewrite the archived SIM14.2 result from the automated wording

> **OUTCOME C — EMBEDDING DEPENDENT: M1 DOES NOT REPRODUCE THE REGULAR \((Z_2)^3\) CARRIER**

—which is correct —into the more mathematically informative truth packet:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
M_1 &: (\mathbb Z_2)^3,\quad PG(2,2),
\\
&\text{nonregular action: }8\rightarrow4+4,
\\[1mm]
M_2 &: (\mathbb Z_2)^3,\quad PG(2,2),
\\
&\text{regular action: }8\rightarrow8.
\end{aligned}}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\textbf{The abstract finite geometry is embedding-stable;}
\quad
\textbf{its eight-state realization is not.}
}
$$

**That**, to me, is SIM14.2.

And now I would absolutely keep the discipline. We shouldn't immediately decide that M2 is the “physical” embedding. But we have just acquired a wonderfully minimal next question:

$$
\boxed{
\textbf{What exact structural condition distinguishes the regular M2 realization from the fractured M1 realization?}
}
$$

We may be able to answer that in SIM14.3 with almost no additional machinery at all. 
